Subject: A memoryless attachment lives in the tiles: none loads it, none stores it

CoreAnimation attaches a second colour attachment to every pass, a texture of
MTLStorageModeMemoryless (com.apple.coreanimation.memoryless-texture): draws
write it and later draws of the same pass read it back by framebuffer fetch.
Its load action is DontCare and its store action is the one a colour
attachment has by default, Store -- which on a GPU that keeps such a texture
in tile memory alone means nothing. Here the texture was given memory like any
other and its store action was obeyed: every tile of the phone's main pass
stored 128 x 128 pixels of it to rows nobody reads (the frame trace of the
home: each pass overwrites what the last one stored, unread).

Texture::setMemoryless (C: hakios_metal_texture_set_memoryless) says what the
texture is. The tiles of a GMEM pass then neither load nor store it, with one
exception: where one pass of the caller's is recorded as two here (a draw that
cannot join the open pass, carrying Load for every attachment as the plug-in
gives every draw of an encoder after its first), the first half stores it and
the second loads it -- closePass is told the pass of the draw that closes it.
A command buffer appended to another keeps it stored, since what follows is
not known there. The texture keeps its rows for the passes drawn in sysmem.

PassTime says which attachments the tiles loaded and stored
(hakios_metal_cb_pass_moved). drawbench's second attachment is now what the
phone's is, memoryless with DontCare and Store, and --no-memoryless makes it an
ordinary texture again. For the main pass of the phone's own programs (scene S,
78 tiles): 78 loads and 156 stores, 4.88 MB read and 9.77 MB written, become
78 and 78, 4.88 MB and 4.88 MB. No golden stream of a scene changes (their
second attachment was DontCare/DontCare before); the new test's line is added.


